In this problem, we need to write two functions: `isEqual` and `contains`.

The function `isEqual` checks if two objects are equal based on their type and values. If both objects are of the same type (e.g., integers or floats), it checks if they are equal using the `equals()` method.

If the objects are not of the same type, it uses a series of if-else statements to check if they are equal based on their types and values.

The function `contains` takes two strings as input and returns true if one string is a substring of another. It does this by calling the `contains()` method on each possible substring.

Here's a breakdown of how you can write these functions in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isEqual(1, 1));  // true
        System.out.println(isEqual(2, 3));  // false

        String s = "test";
        String sub = "te";
        System.out.println(contains(s, sub));  // true
        System.out.println(contains(s, "tes"));  // false
    }

    private static boolean isEqual(Object a, Object b) {
        if (a instanceof Integer && b instanceof Integer) {
            return ((Integer) a).equals((Integer) b);
        } else if (a instanceof Float && b instanceof Float) {
            return ((Float) a).equals((Float) b);
        } else if (a instanceof String && b instanceof String) {
            return ((String) a).toLowerCase().equals((String) b.toLowerCase());
        }
        return false;
    }

    private static boolean contains(String s, String sub) {
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.substring(i, i + sub.length()).contains(sub)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main2(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(contains("test", "te"));  // true
        System.out.println(contains("test", "tes"));  // false
    }
}
```

