Here's a detailed response to your previous question:

The problem asks us to write two functions: `isEqual` and `contains`.

**Function 1: isEqual**

This function checks if two objects are equal based on their type and values.

* If both objects are of the same type (e.g., integers or floats), it calls the `equals()` method on each object.
* If the objects are not of the same type, it uses a series of if-else statements to check if they are equal based on their types and values.

Here's how you can implement this function in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isEqual(1, 1));  // true
        System.out.println(isEqual("hello", "world"));  // false

        String s = "test";
        String sub = "te";
        System.out.println(isEqual(s, sub));  // true
        System.out.println(isEqual(s, "tes"));  // false
    }

    private static boolean isEqual(Object a, Object b) {
        if (a instanceof Integer && b instanceof Integer) {
            return ((Integer) a).equals((Integer) b);
        } else if (a instanceof Float && b instanceof Float) {
            return ((Float) a).equals((Float) b);
        } else if (a instanceof String && b instanceof String) {
            return ((String) a).toLowerCase().equals((String) b.toLowerCase());
        }
        return false;
    }

    private static boolean equals(String s, String sub) {
        for (int i = 0; i < Math.min(s.length(), sub.length()); i++) {
            if (!s.substring(i, i + sub.length()).equals(sub)) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main2(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isEqual(1, 1));  // true
        System.out.println(isEqual("hello", "world"));  // false

        String s = "test";
        String sub = "te";
        System.out.println(isEqual(s, sub));  // true
        System.out.println(isEqual(s, "tes"));  // false
    }
}
```

**Function 2: contains**

This function checks if one string is a substring of another.

* It iterates over each character in the first string and checks if it matches the corresponding character in the second string.
* If it finds a mismatch, it returns false.
* If it finishes checking all characters without finding any mismatches, it returns true.

Here's how you can implement this function in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(contains("test", "te"));  // true
        System.out.println(contains("test", "tes"));  // false

        String s = "hello";
        String sub = "world";
        System.out.println(sub.contains(s));  // true
        System.out.println(sub.contains("worl"));  // false
    }

    private static boolean contains(String s, String sub) {
        for (int i = 0; i < Math.min(s.length(), sub.length()); i++) {
            if (!s.substring(i, i + sub.length()).contains(sub)) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main2(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(contains("test", "te"));  // true
        System.out.println(contains("test", "tes"));  // false

        String s = "hello";
        String sub = "world";
        System.out.println(sub.contains(s));  // true
        System.out.println(sub.contains("worl"));  // false
    }
}
```
