        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main2(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(contains("test", "te"));  // true
        System.out.println(contains("test", "tes"));  // false

        String s = "hello";
        String sub = "world";
        System.out.println(sub.contains(s));  // true
    }
}
```

**Function 3: equals**

This function checks if two objects are equal based on their type and values.

* It uses the `equals()` method to compare the objects.
* If it finds a mismatch, it returns false.
* If it finishes checking all objects without finding any mismatches, it returns true.

Here's how you can implement this function in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(equals(new Object(), new Object()));  // true
        System.out.println(equals(1, 2));  // false

        String s = "test";
        Integer a = new Integer(10);
        int b = new Integer(20);
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));  // false
    }

    private static boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (o instanceof Object[]) {
            Object[] arr = (Object[]) o;
            return arr[0].equals(arr[1]);
        } else if (o instanceof int[]) {
            int[] a = (int[]) o;
            return a[0] == a[1];
        } else if (o instanceof String[]) {
            String[] sArr = (String[]) o;
            return Arrays.equals(sArr, Arrays.asList(o).toArray());
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main2(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(equals(new Object(), new Object()));  // true
        System.out.println(equals(1, 2));  // false

        String s = "test";
        Integer a = new Integer(10);
        int b = new Integer(20);
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));  // false
    }
}
```

Note that the `equals()` function in Java checks for reference equality, meaning it compares two objects by their memory address. If you want to compare objects based on their actual values, you can use the `hashCode()` method instead.

**Function 4: hashCode**

This function returns a hash code value for an object.

* It uses the `hashCode()` method to get the hash code.
* If it finds a mismatch, it returns null or throws an exception.
* If it finishes checking all objects without finding any mismatches, it returns the calculated hash code.

Here's how you can implement this function in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(hashCode(new Object()));  // int

        String s = "test";
        Integer a = new Integer(10);
        int b = new Integer(20);

        System.out.println(a.hashCode());  // some integer value
        System.out.println(b.hashCode());  // some other integer value
    }

    private static int hashCode(Object o) {
        if (o instanceof String[]) {
            return Arrays.hashCode((String[]) o);
        } else if (o instanceof int[]) {
            return Arrays.hashCode(o);
        }
        return 0;
    }

    public static void main2(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(hashCode(new Object()));  // int

        String s = "test";
        Integer a = new Integer(10);
        int b = new Integer(20);

        System.out.println(a.hashCode());  // some integer value
        System.out.println(b.hashCode());  // some other integer value
    }
}
```

